; 09a-prove-an-existential-statement.kurt
;
; Prove an existential statement
;
; To prove `∃ x P(x)`, give a witness: an object `t` for which `P(t)` holds. Prove `P(t)` for it,
; then write the existential. Kurt closes the step with "exists-intro" of logic.kurt: from an
; statement about `t`, `t` may be replaced by a bound variable. (Lesson 09 is the other direction:
; to *use* an existential you have, `pick` a witness.)

load logic, number
calc on              ; for evaluating `3 > 2` and `3 * 3` below

; 1. The witness is known to have the property: a fact about it, then the existential.

prop P
const c
use P(c)

∃ x P(x)                          ; by exists-intro: `c` is the witness

; 2. Find the witness first, on scratch paper: is there a number between 2 and 4? Yes, 3. The proof
; is the check that it has the property, and then the existential.

show ∃ x (x > 2 ∧ x < 4)
proof
    3 > 2 ∧ 3 < 4                ; the witness 3 has both properties
    ∃ x (x > 2 ∧ x < 4)          ; so there is such an x
qed

; 3. The witness may be a computed term, here `3` is found as the number whose square is 9.

show ∃ x (x * x = 9)
proof
    3 * 3 = 9
    ∃ x (x * x = 9)
qed

; The witness can get a name with `def`, inside the proof. The name is local to the proof: it is
; gone when the proof closes, and the claim may not mention it (it doesn't: `∃ x` hides the witness).

show ∃ x (x * x = 16)
proof
    def t = 4                    ; `t` names the witness, in this proof only
    t * t = 4 * 4
    t * t = 16
    ∃ x (x * x = 16)             ; `t` is the witness
qed

; A named witness helps when it is a term about an object of the proof. For every `a` there is a
; number larger than `a * a`, for example `a * a + 1` -- it depends on `a`, the arbitrary object of a
; `let` (lesson 08), so its `def` stands inside the `let`:

show ∀ $a ∃ x (x > $a * $a)
proof
    let a
        def w = a * a + 1        ; the witness, a term about `a`
        0 < 1
        0 + a * a < 1 + a * a
        a * a < a * a + 1
        a * a < w
        ∃ x (x > a * a)          ; `w` is the witness
qed

; 4. With a condition: `∃ x > 2 (x < 4)` means `∃ x (x > 2 ∧ x < 4)`. Kurt wants the condition and
; the body for the witness, as one statement:

show ∃ x > 2 (x < 4)
proof
    3 > 2 ∧ 3 < 4
    ∃ x > 2 (x < 4)
qed

; 5. Two quantifiers: one witness for each, the inner one first.

show ∃ x ∃ y (x + y = 5)
proof
    2 + 3 = 5                     ; x = 2, y = 3
    ∃ y (2 + y = 5)               ; the witness for y
    ∃ x ∃ y (x + y = 5)           ; the witness for x
qed

; 6. The witness can be an object of the proof itself, for example one from a `pick`: from an
; object with `P` and `Q`, there is an object with `P`.

prop Q
use ∃ y (P(y) ∧ Q)

show ∃ z P(z)
proof
    pick b with P(b) ∧ Q
        P(b)
        ∃ z P(z)                  ; `b` is the witness
qed

; The `pick` block is left with `∃ z P(z)`, which does not mention `b`; so it is fine (lesson 09).

; 7. A witness that does not work is rejected. `3` is not larger than 5:

expect "ProofError"
    show ∃ x (x > 5 ∧ x < 4)
    proof
        3 > 5 ∧ 3 < 4
    qed

; Nothing says which witness is the right one: Kurt only checks the one you give. And there is
; nothing to prove about `x` itself: `∃ x P(x)` follows from one `P(t)`. To prove that *every* x
; has a property, a `let` is needed instead (lesson 08).

; 8. Several variables are written as a list: `∃ x, y P` is `∃ x ∃ y P`, and each name may have its own
; condition (`∀ x > 0, y > 0 P` is `∀ x > 0 ∀ y > 0 P`). The witnesses are two, the proof is the same:

show ∃ a, b (a < b)
proof
    1 < 2
    ∃ b (1 < b)                  ; (witness 2 for b)
    ∃ a, b (a < b)               ; (witness 1 for a)
qed

; The other direction, lesson 09, takes the witnesses apart with one `pick`: `pick x, y with F`.
